\description{
Build a ready-to-be-used \code{missing_taxa_list} object based on taxonomic information from the backbone phylogeny and the taxa to graft.
The \code{missing_taxa_list} can then be used as input for \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{phylograftR::graft_missing_taxa()}} or \code{\link[=graft_missing_clades]{phylograftR::graft_missing_clades()}}.

Target clades, sister clades and excluded subclades are identified with taxonomic names (e.g., \code{"Homo"}) or with taxonomic levels (e.g., \code{"Genus"}) instead of tips or node IDs.
The function retrieves the MRCA node of each taxonomic group in the backbone phylogeny, checks that the group is monophyletic,
and writes each clade as a pair of tips, as expected by \code{\link[=build_missing_taxa_list]{phylograftR::build_missing_taxa_list()}}.

Taxonomic tables are the go-to option for large phylogenies with neatly defined taxonomic levels (see 'When to use taxonomic tables' in Details).
Use \code{\link[=import_taxonomic_table]{phylograftR::import_taxonomic_table()}} to read the tables directly from files.

For a finer level of control on the clades to use as target clades, sister clades, and/or excluded subclades, you can use alternative functions
that define those clades based on any node IDs or pairs of tips used to identify MRCA:
see \code{\link[=build_missing_taxa_list]{phylograftR::build_missing_taxa_list()}} and \code{\link[=import_missing_taxa_list]{phylograftR::import_missing_taxa_list()}}.
}
\details{
\strong{When to use taxonomic tables}

Taxonomic tables are the go-to option for large phylogenies with neatly defined taxonomic levels (e.g., Family, Subfamily, Tribe, Genus). The taxonomy of the backbone is provided once,
and each taxon to graft only needs its own taxonomic levels: the constraints (target clade, sister clade, excluded subclades) are written with the names of the groups or of the taxonomic levels to use,
and the clades are retrieved from the phylogeny. The same group is therefore always the same clade, whatever the number of taxa, which avoids the typos and
inconsistencies of clades written taxon by taxon, and the tables can be taken as such from a taxonomic database.

The limit is that only the taxonomic groups of the backbone table can be used as clades. To fine-tune the grafting of some taxa with any other node of the phylogeny
(e.g., a clade without a name, a part of a group, or a group of several genera), define their clades by tips or node IDs with
\code{\link[=build_missing_taxa_list]{phylograftR::build_missing_taxa_list()}} or \code{\link[=import_missing_taxa_list]{phylograftR::import_missing_taxa_list()}} (see 'Fine-tuning the clades' in their Details sections).
Both routes return the same \code{missing_taxa_list}, so a list built from taxonomic tables can be adjusted afterwards.

\strong{Backbone taxonomic table}

Names of taxonomic levels (the column names; e.g., \code{"Genus"}) and names of taxonomic groups (their values; e.g., \code{"Homo"}) must be unambiguous:
\itemize{
\item A level name cannot be the name of a tip, of a taxon to graft, or of a group.
\item A group name belongs to a single level: the same name cannot be found in two columns (e.g., a Family cannot be named the same as a Genus).
\item Level names and group names cannot contain \code{";"}. Level names cannot be \code{"Taxa"}, \code{"Type"} or the names of the other columns of \code{missing_taxa_taxonomic_table}.
\item All the tips of \code{backbone_taxonomic_table} must be in \code{phy}. Tips of \code{phy} absent from the table are allowed (with a warning).
}

\strong{Missing taxa taxonomic table}
\itemize{
\item \code{Taxa} (Required): Names of the taxa to graft. Names must be unique and absent from \code{phy$tip.label}.
\item \code{Type} (Required): \code{"taxa"} for taxa grafted with \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{phylograftR::graft_missing_taxa()}} within a target clade,
or \code{"clades"} for taxa grafted with \code{\link[=graft_missing_clades]{phylograftR::graft_missing_clades()}} as a sister of a clade.
\item Taxonomic levels (Optional): Same columns as in \code{backbone_taxonomic_table}. They are used to retrieve the taxonomic group of the taxon when \code{Target_clade}, \code{Sister_clade},
and/or \code{Excluded_subclades} (see below) use a taxonomic level (e.g., \code{"Genus"}) rather than directly a taxonomic group (e.g., \code{"Homo"}) as identification.
Columns that are not levels of the backbone, nor one of the columns listed here, are ignored with a warning.
\item \code{Target_clade} (Required for \code{Type = "taxa"}) or \code{Sister_clade} (Required for \code{Type = "clades"}): A single taxonomic group, written either as the name of the group
(e.g., \code{"Homo"}), or as the name of a taxonomic level (e.g., \code{"Genus"}). If a taxonomic level is provided, the group will be identified as recorded in the column of the same name
in \code{missing_taxa_taxonomic_table} (e.g., \code{"Genus"} with \code{Genus = "Camponotus"} gives the clade of the genus \code{Camponotus}).
It is an error if the group is absent from the backbone, or if the taxon has no value at the requested level.
\item \code{Excluded_subclades} (Optional, \code{Type = "taxa"}): Subclades of the target clade where the taxon is known not to belong, separated by semicolons (e.g., \code{"Pheidole; Camponotus"}).
Each one is a group or a level name, as in \code{Target_clade}.
\item \verb{$include_target_clade_stem} (Optional, \code{Type = "taxa"}): Logical. Whether the stem branch of the target clade is a valid placement to graft this taxa (\code{NA} uses the global argument set in the grafting function).
\item \verb{$exclude_subclades_stem} (Optional, \code{Type = "taxa"}): Logical. Whether the stem branch of the excluded clade(s) is/are a excluded from grafting too. Separated by semicolons if multiple \code{Excluded_subclades} are provided
(\code{NA} uses the global argument set in the grafting function).
\item \verb{$allowed_states} (Optional): For each taxon, the states of a \code{simmap} in which it can be grafted
(see the \code{simmap} argument of \code{\link[=graft_missing_taxa]{phylograftR::graft_missing_taxa()}} or \code{\link[=graft_missing_clades]{phylograftR::graft_missing_clades()}}), separated by commas.
If multiple traits are used, each set of states must be separated by semicolons (e.g., \code{"blue, red; low"}).
It is accepted with both \code{Target_clade} and \code{Sister_clade}. (\code{NA} uses the global argument set in the grafting function).
}

\strong{Non-monophyletic groups}

What to do when a taxonomic group is not monophyletic in \code{phy}, i.e., when the clade defined by the MRCA of its tips also holds tips of other groups of the same level?
\itemize{
\item \code{non_monophyletic = "exclude"} (default): For target clades, intruders (i.e., tips from other groups descending from the MRCA) are added to the
\code{Excluded_subclades} of the taxon (with their stem, \code{exclude_subclades_stem = TRUE}), so the taxon is never grafted among intruders.
Intruders nested within an excluded subclade provided by the user are not added.
Intruders cannot be removed from a sister clade or from a group of \code{Excluded_subclades}: the clade of the MRCA is used with a warning (as for \code{"mrca"}).
For \code{Excluded_subclades}, all the branches descending from the MRCA are then excluded, including the ones of intruders.
\item \code{non_monophyletic = "mrca"}: The clade of the MRCA is used as such, with a warning, including its intruders.
\item \code{non_monophyletic = "error"}: Stop if one group used is not monophyletic.
}
}
\examples{
## Load the toy backbone phylogeny and its taxonomy
data(toy_backbone)
data(toy_backbone_taxonomic_table)
head(toy_backbone_taxonomic_table)

## Load the taxonomic table of the taxa to graft
data(toy_missing_taxa_taxonomic_table)
toy_missing_taxa_taxonomic_table

# The taxa to graft with graft_missing_taxa() (Type = "taxa") use different types of constraints:
# * Genus_a_sp1: grafted in its family (Target_clade = "Family" is the family of the taxon, i.e., "Family_A"),
#   but not in the subfamily "Subfam_A1" nor in the tribe "Tribe_A2a" (Excluded_subclades).
#   The stem of the family is available (include_target_clade_stem),
#   the stem of "Subfam_A1" is excluded too, but not the one of "Tribe_A2a" (exclude_subclades_stem).
# * Genus_a_sp2: same target clade, but excluded subclades are a tribe and a genus with a single species ("Gen_3"),
    and the stem is excluded for the genus only.
# * Genus_a_sp3: same target clade, without excluded subclades and
    without access to the stem of the family.
# * Genus_b_sp1: grafted in the family "Family_B", written directly with the name of the group.
# * Genus_b_sp2: grafted in its subfamily (Target_clade = "Subfamily", i.e., "Subfam_B1"),
    except in the genus "Gen_1".
# * Genus_c_sp1: grafted on the terminal branch of the only species
    of the genus "Gen_2" (a single tip).
# * Genus_c_sp2: grafted in its tribe (Target_clade = "Tribe", i.e., "Tribe_B2"),
    without access to the stem.

# The taxa to graft with graft_missing_clades() (Type = "clades") use sister clades:
# * Genus_d_sp1 and Genus_d_sp2: belong to a new genus, and have the same sister clade ("Gen_1"),
#   so they are grafted together as a missing clade.
# * Genus_e_sp1: grafted as the sister of its own tribe (Sister_clade = "Tribe", i.e., "Tribe_A1b").

## Build the missing taxa lists associated to these taxonomic tables
missing_taxa_lists <- build_missing_taxa_list_from_taxonomy(
  backbone_taxonomic_table = toy_backbone_taxonomic_table,
  missing_taxa_taxonomic_table = toy_missing_taxa_taxonomic_table,
  phy = toy_backbone)

# Since both types of taxa to graft were provided,
# the output contains one list per grafting function
names(missing_taxa_lists)
str(missing_taxa_lists$taxa, max.level = 1) # Missing_taxa_list for graft_missing_taxa()
str(missing_taxa_lists$clades, max.level = 1) # Missing_taxa_list for graft_missing_clades()

# Clades are written as pairs of tips, and named after their taxonomic groups
missing_taxa_lists$taxa$Target_clade
missing_taxa_lists$taxa$Excluded_subclades$Genus_a_sp1
missing_taxa_lists$taxa$exclude_subclades_stem$Genus_a_sp1
missing_taxa_lists$clades$Sister_clade

# The taxa to graft as clades share their sister clade, so they will be grafted together
missing_taxa_lists$clades$Sister_clade_name

# Information on the groups used
attr(missing_taxa_lists, "taxonomic_groups")

# The lists can be used for grafting
grafted_taxa <- graft_missing_taxa(toy_backbone, missing_taxa_lists$taxa, verbose = FALSE)
grafted_clades <- graft_missing_clades(toy_backbone, missing_taxa_lists$clades, verbose = FALSE)

}
